```java
@Component
public class UserAccountLookup implements AccountLookup {
    private final UserAccountRepository repository;

    public UserAccountLookup(UserAccountRepository repository) {
        this.repository = repository;
    }

    @Override
    public Optional<UserAccount> findById(Integer id) {
        return repository.findById(id);
    }
}
```

Spring injects all handlers into the service:

```java
@Service
public class AccountService {
    private final List<AccountLookup> lookups;

    public AccountService(List<AccountLookup> lookups) {
        this.lookups = lookups;
    }

    @Transactional(readOnly = true)
    public Account getById(Integer id) {
        Objects.requireNonNull(id, "id must not be null");

        for (AccountLookup lookup : lookups) {
            Optional<? extends Account> account = lookup.findById(id);
            if (account.isPresent()) {
                return account.get();
            }
        }

        throw new EntityNotFoundException("Account not found: " + id);
    }
}
```

Imports are omitted; use `jakarta.persistence.EntityNotFoundException` for Spring Boot 3+, or the corresponding `javax.persistence` class for older versions.

Adding a new account type requires its entity, repository, and lookup bean, but no changes to `AccountService`.

**Trade-offs:** This performs up to one query per account type. For many types, a central account table or an `account ID → type` registry is more efficient. Also, independent table primary keys do **not** enforce cross-table uniqueness; maintain that guarantee explicitly. At an HTTP boundary, return a DTO that excludes `passwordHash`, rather than serializing the entity directly.

