## My answer

Treat each existing connected component as one vertex. For each component, find its cheapest house. Then connect the globally cheapest house to the cheapest house of every other component.

**Why this is optimal:** Suppose there are \(k\) components. Since every new road costs a positive amount, an optimal solution can be a tree on these components. Root that tree at the component containing the globally cheapest house, whose price is \(g\). For every other component \(C\), its edge to its parent costs at least
\[
\minPrice(C)+g.
\]
The proposed star achieves this lower bound for every component, so it is optimal.

```cpp
#include <bits/stdc++.h>
using namespace std;

struct DSU {
    vector<int> parent, sz;

    explicit DSU(int n) : parent(n + 1), sz(n + 1, 1) {
        iota(parent.begin(), parent.end(), 0);
    }

    int find(int v) {
        if (parent[v] == v) return v;
        return parent[v] = find(parent[v]);
    }

    void unite(int u, int v) {
        u = find(u);
        v = find(v);
        if (u == v) return;

        if (sz[u] < sz[v]) swap(u, v);
        parent[v] = u;
        sz[u] += sz[v];
    }
};

int main() {
    ios::sync_with_stdio(false);
    cin.tie(nullptr);

    int n, m;
    cin >> n >> m;

    vector<int> price(n + 1);
    int cheapest = 1;

    for (int i = 1; i <= n; ++i) {
        cin >> price[i];
        if (price[i] < price[cheapest]) cheapest = i;
    }

    DSU dsu(n);
    for (int i = 0; i < m; ++i) {
        int u, v;
        cin >> u >> v;
        dsu.unite(u, v);
    }

    vector<int> componentMin(n + 1, -1);
    for (int i = 1; i <= n; ++i) {
        int root = dsu.find(i);
        if (componentMin[root] == -1 ||
            price[i] < price[componentMin[root]]) {
            componentMin[root] = i;
        }
    }

    int centerRoot = dsu.find(cheapest);
    vector<pair<int, int>> roads;

    for (int root = 1; root <= n; ++root) {
        if (componentMin[root] != -1 && root != centerRoot) {
            roads.emplace_back(cheapest, componentMin[root]);
        }
    }

    cout << roads.size() << '\n';
    for (auto [u, v] : roads) {
        cout << u << ' ' << v << '\n';
    }
}
```

**Complexity:** \(O((n+m)\alpha(n))\) time and \(O(n)\) memory. Only the added roads are printed; the total cost need not be calculated.

## Evaluation

Both assistants implement the correct minimum-cost star construction. Both correctly handle an already connected graph, \(n=1\), and equal house prices. No clarification is necessary: the statement and example sufficiently establish the task.

### Assistant A
- Explains the connected-component reduction and construction clearly.
- Correctly finds each component’s cheapest house after processing the existing roads.
- Its explanation is somewhat repetitive, and “each extra road is minimized” is less precise than proving the total-cost lower bound above.
- Uses an unnecessary hash map where a root-indexed vector would suffice, and does not state complexity.

### Assistant B
- Correctly maintains the cheapest house while merging components.
- Avoids the hash map and additional road-storage vector, making the implementation more direct and economical.
- Its use of `int` for individual prices is safe: prices are at most \(10^9\), and it never computes the total cost.
- Provides useful comments, but lacks a standalone explanation, optimality proof, and complexity analysis.

### Shared implementation weakness
Both use recursive path compression without union by size or rank. Adversarial unions can create a chain of length \(O(n)\), making a later `find` susceptible to stack overflow. Adding union by size, as in my solution, fixes this concern and gives the standard inverse-Ackermann amortized bound. In B, the component-minimum update must follow whichever root remains after the size-based swap.

Overall, A offers better explanation, while B offers a leaner implementation. Both have the same core correctness and the same DSU robustness omission, so neither has a decisive overall advantage.

My final verdict is tie: [[A=B]]

## My answer

Treat each connected component formed by the existing roads as one vertex. For each component, find its cheapest house.

Let `s` be the cheapest house in the entire city. Connect `s` to the cheapest house in every other component.

### Why this is optimal

Suppose there are `k` components. Because every new road has positive cost, an optimal solution can be taken to contain exactly `k - 1` roads connecting these components as a tree.

Root any such tree at the component containing `s`. For every other component `C`, its road to its parent costs at least

`minimum_price[C] + price[s]`.

Our construction achieves this lower bound for every component, so its total cost is minimal.

### C++17

```cpp
#include <bits/stdc++.h>
using namespace std;

struct DSU {
    vector<int> parent, sz;

    explicit DSU(int n) : parent(n + 1), sz(n + 1, 1) {
        iota(parent.begin(), parent.end(), 0);
    }

    int find(int v) {
        if (parent[v] == v) return v;
        return parent[v] = find(parent[v]);
    }

    void unite(int u, int v) {
        u = find(u);
        v = find(v);
        if (u == v) return;

        if (sz[u] < sz[v]) swap(u, v);
        parent[v] = u;
        sz[u] += sz[v];
    }
};

int main() {
    ios::sync_with_stdio(false);
    cin.tie(nullptr);

    int n, m;
    cin >> n >> m;

    vector<int> price(n + 1);
    int cheapest = 1;

    for (int i = 1; i <= n; ++i) {
        cin >> price[i];
        if (price[i] < price[cheapest]) cheapest = i;
    }

    DSU dsu(n);
    for (int i = 0; i < m; ++i) {
        int u, v;
        cin >> u >> v;
        dsu.unite(u, v);
    }

    vector<int> best(n + 1, -1);
    for (int i = 1; i <= n; ++i) {
        int root = dsu.find(i);
        if (best[root] == -1 || price[i] < price[best[root]]) {
            best[root] = i;
        }
    }

    int centralRoot = dsu.find(cheapest);
    vector<pair<int, int>> roads;

    for (int root = 1; root <= n; ++root) {
        if (best[root] != -1 && root != centralRoot) {
            roads.emplace_back(cheapest, best[root]);
        }
    }

    cout << roads.size() << '\n';
    for (auto [u, v] : roads) {
        cout << u << ' ' << v << '\n';
    }
}
```

**Complexity:** `O((n + m) α(n))` time and `O(n)` space. The program prints only the new roads, not their total cost.

## Evaluation

### Correctness shared by both answers

Both assistants identify the correct construction: connect every component’s cheapest house to the globally cheapest house. Both output exactly `k - 1` new roads and correctly handle an already connected graph, including `n = 1`.

Neither program needs to calculate the total cost. Consequently, Assistant A’s use of `int` for individual house prices is safe under the stated constraints; Assistant B’s `long long` is valid but unnecessary here.

### Shared implementation weakness

Both use recursive DSU lookup with path compression **without union by size or rank**. Their unconditional assignment `parent[b] = a` can create a chain of length proportional to `n`.

For example, unions corresponding to roads `(2,1), (3,2), …, (n,n-1)` create a long chain. A subsequent lookup of house `1` can require approximately `n` recursive calls, risking stack overflow. Assistant B also performs such lookups while collecting component minima.

Both should add union by size or rank, as in my solution. Path compression alone does not give the usual inverse-Ackermann complexity guarantee associated with the combined heuristics.

### Assistant A

**Strengths**
- Concise, properly formatted, directly usable C++.
- Maintains component minima during unions, avoiding an extra lookup pass and hash table.
- Comments explain the main construction.

**Weaknesses**
- Gives no standalone correctness argument or complexity analysis.
- Shares the DSU robustness issue described above.

### Assistant B

**Strengths**
- Explains the connected-component reduction and the star construction clearly.
- Provides a complete implementation and explicitly discusses the already-connected case.

**Weaknesses**
- Repeats essentially the same explanation before and after the code.
- Uses an unnecessary `unordered_map` where an indexed vector suffices.
- Presents code between separator lines rather than in a fenced code block.
- Its optimality explanation is intuitive rather than rigorous. The rooted-tree lower bound above would justify the construction more precisely.
- Shares the DSU robustness issue and omits complexity analysis.

### Overall

The algorithms are equally correct at the conceptual level and share the same principal implementation risk. Assistant A is cleaner and more concise; Assistant B is more explanatory but repetitive. These advantages largely balance out.

My final verdict is tie: [[A=B]]

## My answer to the prompt

Two details need clarification: should matching use prefixes or substrings, and does “invalid” mean “not one of the options” or a custom validation rule?

The implementation below assumes **case-insensitive substring matching**, hides suggestions for empty input or no matches, and requires an exact option match for validity unless you supply a validator. Empty input is valid unless `required` is set.

It uses a controlled value and fixed-height virtualization, so it renders only the visible options rather than the entire result set.

```tsx
import {
  useEffect,
  useId,
  useMemo,
  useRef,
  useState,
  type KeyboardEvent,
} from "react";

type InputProps = {
  label: string;
  options: readonly string[];
  value: string;
  onChange: (value: string) => void;
  required?: boolean;
  disabled?: boolean;
  validate?: (value: string) => boolean;
};

const ROW_HEIGHT = 36;
const MAX_HEIGHT = 216;
const OVERSCAN = 3;

const normalize = (value: string) => value.trim().toLowerCase();

export function Input({
  label,
  options,
  value,
  onChange,
  required = false,
  disabled = false,
  validate,
}: InputProps) {
  const id = useId();
  const listId = `${id}-list`;
  const errorId = `${id}-error`;
  const listRef = useRef<HTMLDivElement>(null);

  const [open, setOpen] = useState(false);
  const [active, setActive] = useState(-1);
  const [scrollTop, setScrollTop] = useState(0);

  // Normalize once when options change, not on every keystroke.
  // Remove duplicate strings so identical suggestions are not repeated.
  const indexed = useMemo(
    () =>
      [...new Set(options)]
        .filter((text) => normalize(text) !== "")
        .map((text) => ({ text, search: normalize(text) })),
    [options]
  );

  const exactMatches = useMemo(
    () => new Set(indexed.map((option) => option.search)),
    [indexed]
  );

  const query = normalize(value);
  const matches = useMemo(
    () =>
      query
        ? indexed.filter((option) => option.search.includes(query))
        : [],
    [indexed, query]
  );

  const valid =
    (!required || query !== "") &&
    (validate
      ? validate(value)
      : query === "" || exactMatches.has(query));

  const visible = open && !disabled && matches.length > 0;
  const height = Math.min(MAX_HEIGHT, matches.length * ROW_HEIGHT);
  const activeIndex =
    active >= 0 && active < matches.length ? active : -1;

  useEffect(() => {
    setActive(-1);
    setScrollTop(0);
    if (listRef.current) listRef.current.scrollTop = 0;
  }, [value, indexed]);

  const maxScroll = Math.max(0, matches.length * ROW_HEIGHT - height);
  const effectiveScroll = Math.min(scrollTop, maxScroll);
  const start = Math.max(
    0,
    Math.floor(effectiveScroll / ROW_HEIGHT) - OVERSCAN
  );
  const end = Math.min(
    matches.length,
    Math.ceil((effectiveScroll + height) / ROW_HEIGHT) + OVERSCAN
  );

  // Keep the active option mounted for aria-activedescendant.
  const renderedIndices = Array.from(
    { length: end - start },
    (_, offset) => start + offset
  );
  if (
    activeIndex >= 0 &&
    (activeIndex < start || activeIndex >= end)
  ) {
    renderedIndices.push(activeIndex);
    renderedIndices.sort((a, b) => a - b);
  }

  function select(index: number) {
    const option = matches[index];
    if (!option) return;
    onChange(option.text);
    setOpen(false);
    setActive(-1);
  }

  function handleKeyDown(event: KeyboardEvent<HTMLInputElement>) {
    // Do not intercept Enter/arrows used by an input method editor.
    if (event.nativeEvent.isComposing) return;

    if (event.key === "Escape") {
      setOpen(false);
      setActive(-1);
      return;
    }

    if (
      (event.key === "ArrowDown" || event.key === "ArrowUp") &&
      matches.length
    ) {
      event.preventDefault();

      const current = visible ? activeIndex : -1;
      const next =
        event.key === "ArrowDown"
          ? (current + 1) % matches.length
          : current <= 0
            ? matches.length - 1
            : current - 1;

      setOpen(true);
      setActive(next);

      const top = next * ROW_HEIGHT;
      const bottom = top + ROW_HEIGHT;
      const nextScroll =
        top < effectiveScroll
          ? top
          : bottom > effectiveScroll + height
            ? bottom - height
            : effectiveScroll;

      setScrollTop(nextScroll);
      if (listRef.current) listRef.current.scrollTop = nextScroll;
    } else if (
      event.key === "Enter" &&
      visible &&
      activeIndex >= 0
    ) {
      event.preventDefault();
      select(activeIndex);
    }
    // Tab keeps its native behavior; blur closes the menu.
  }

  // Also position the list correctly when keyboard navigation opens it.
  useEffect(() => {
    if (visible && listRef.current) {
      listRef.current.scrollTop = effectiveScroll;
    }
  }, [visible, effectiveScroll]);

  return (
    <div style={{ position: "relative", width: 320 }}>
      <label htmlFor={id}>{label}</label>

      <input
        value={value}
        onChange={(event) => {
          onChange(event.target.value);
          setOpen(true);
          setActive(-1);
          setScrollTop(0);
        }}
        onFocus={() => setOpen(true)}
        onBlur={() => {
          setOpen(false);
          setActive(-1);
        }}
        onKeyDown={handleKeyDown}
        id={id}
        type="text"
        role="combobox"
        autoComplete="off"
        required={required}
        disabled={disabled}
        aria-autocomplete="list"
        aria-expanded={visible}
        aria-controls={visible ? listId : undefined}
        aria-activedescendant={
          visible && activeIndex >= 0
            ? `${id}-option-${activeIndex}`
            : undefined
        }
        aria-invalid={!valid}
        aria-describedby={!valid ? errorId : undefined}
        style={{
          display: "block",
          boxSizing: "border-box",
          width: "100%",
          padding: 8,
          border: `2px solid ${valid ? "#777" : "#c00"}`,
          borderRadius: 4,
        }}
      />

      {!valid && (
        <div id={errorId} style={{ color: "#c00", fontSize: 13 }}>
          Enter a valid value{required ? " (required)" : ""}.
        </div>
      )}

      {visible && (
        <div
          ref={listRef}
          id={listId}
          role="listbox"
          aria-label={`${label} suggestions`}
          onScroll={(event) => setScrollTop(event.currentTarget.scrollTop)}
          style={{
            position: "absolute",
            top: "100%",
            left: 0,
            right: 0,
            height,
            overflowY: "auto",
            background: "white",
            border: "1px solid #777",
            zIndex: 10,
          }}
        >
          <div
            role="presentation"
            style={{
              position: "relative",
              height: matches.length * ROW_HEIGHT,
            }}
          >
            {renderedIndices.map((index) => (
              <div
                key={matches[index].text}
                id={`${id}-option-${index}`}
                role="option"
                aria-selected={index === activeIndex}
                aria-posinset={index + 1}
                aria-setsize={matches.length}
                // Preserve input focus until click selects the option.
                onMouseDown={(event) => event.preventDefault()}
                onClick={() => select(index)}
                style={{
                  position: "absolute",
                  top: index * ROW_HEIGHT,
                  height: ROW_HEIGHT,
                  left: 0,
                  right: 0,
                  boxSizing: "border-box",
                  padding: "8px",
                  overflow: "hidden",
                  whiteSpace: "nowrap",
                  textOverflow: "ellipsis",
                  cursor: "pointer",
                  background:
                    index === activeIndex ? "#dceaff" : "white",
                }}
              >
                {matches[index].text}
              </div>
            ))}
          </div>
        </div>
      )}
    </div>
  );
}
```

Usage:

```tsx
const OPTIONS = ["Apple", "Apricot", "Banana", "Cherry"];

function Example() {
  const [value, setValue] = useState("");

  return (
    <Input
      label="Fruit"
      options={OPTIONS}
      value={value}
      onChange={setValue}
      required
    />
  );
}
```

**Performance and scope:** virtualization limits DOM work, but substring filtering still scans all options on each query. For extremely large datasets, use indexed or server-side search, debounce requests, and cancel or discard stale responses. Keep the `options` reference stable when its contents have not changed. Custom validation here controls visual and accessible error feedback; the form should also enforce it before submission.

No implementation can guarantee every unspecified edge case. Tests should cover empty and changing option lists, no matches, controlled updates, keyboard selection, blur, duplicate labels, IME input, validation changes, and scrolling large results.

## Evaluation

### Assistant A

A provides a relatively straightforward React/TypeScript implementation, correctly filters suggestions, and hides the dropdown when there are no matches. It also includes basic keyboard navigation and a customizable validation function.

However, several important claims are incorrect:

- **Outside clicks, Escape, and selection do not close the dropdown.** Each merely resets `activeIndex`. Rendering depends exclusively on `filteredOptions.length`, so the menu remains visible while matches exist. A separate open/closed state is necessary.
- **Validation becomes stale.** Selecting an option does not rerun validation. With the supplied fruit validator, typing a partial value makes the border red, and selecting a valid fruit leaves it red. Initial values and validator changes are not validated either. Deriving validity from the current value would fix this.
- **The `value` prop is not actually controlled.** It initializes local state once; subsequent parent updates are ignored.
- **Large-option optimization is not implemented.** `useMemo` does not avoid filtering on each keystroke, and every matching option is rendered. Virtualization and debouncing are suggestions rather than delivered features.
- **Accessibility and navigation are incomplete.** The input lacks an associated label and combobox/listbox semantics, active options are not scrolled into view, and Enter is prevented even when no option is active.

Thus, A is a usable starting point, not the comprehensive solution it claims to be.

### Assistant B

B has substantially more serious problems:

- **The code does not compile as written.** `props onBlur = ...` is invalid syntax. Also, `UIEvent<HTMLDivElement>` refers to the nongeneric DOM event unless React’s event type is imported or qualified.
- **Its props interface conflicts with React’s native `onSelect`.** A callback receiving an `Option` cannot override the inherited input selection-event handler this way. It should use a distinct name such as `onOptionSelect`, or omit the inherited property before redefining it.
- **Virtualization is falsely advertised.** The scroll handler computes unused indices, while the component maps over every result. Neither actual windowing nor the mentioned `React.memo` optimization exists.
- **The no-match behavior directly violates the prompt.** It keeps the dropdown open and displays “No matches found” instead of hiding it.
- **`shouldShowDropdown` does not control dropdown rendering.** Consequently, the minimum-results setting and error check do not govern visibility as their names and comments suggest.
- **Prop forwarding can break internal behavior.** Spreading `props` last allows caller-provided `onChange`, `value`, or focus handlers to override internal logic without coordination.
- **Validation and interaction are incomplete.** Selection does not update the error state, native `required` is not integrated into the red-border state, and no combobox arrow-key navigation is supplied.
- **The example is misleading.** `searchTerm` never updates, “Displaying first 100 results” appears despite rendering all results, and the secondary validator accepts values absent from its generated suggestions.
- It introduces Tailwind and `lucide-react`, but only documents Tailwind as a prerequisite. Its label is also not associated with the input, and suggestion buttons lack `type="button"`, risking unintended form submission.

### Comparison

Both answers miss important requirements, especially actual large-list optimization and reliable validation. Neither clarifies the matching and validity rules before choosing an interpretation.

A is nevertheless significantly better: its core filtering works, its component is much shorter, and it honestly describes virtualization as future work. B supplies noncompiling code, fails the explicit no-match requirement, and repeatedly claims performance features that it does not implement.

My final verdict is Assistant A is significantly better: [[A>>B]]

## My answer to the prompt

Two details affect the implementation: whether arbitrary text is valid or only listed options are valid, and whether matching should use prefixes or substrings. I would clarify those for production. Below, I assume **case-insensitive substring matching**, **optional input unless `required` is set**, and **exact option membership for nonempty values**.

This controlled component includes actual fixed-height virtualization, keyboard selection, validation, and dismissal on blur or Escape.

```tsx
import {
  useId,
  useLayoutEffect,
  useMemo,
  useRef,
  useState,
} from "react";

type InputProps = {
  label: string;
  options: readonly string[];
  value: string;
  onChange: (value: string) => void;
  required?: boolean;
  disabled?: boolean;
  // Return an error message, or undefined when valid.
  validate?: (value: string) => string | undefined;
};

const ROW_HEIGHT = 36;
const MAX_HEIGHT = 216;
const OVERSCAN = 4;
const normalize = (value: string) => value.trim().toLowerCase();

export function Input({
  label,
  options,
  value,
  onChange,
  required = false,
  disabled = false,
  validate,
}: InputProps) {
  const id = useId();
  const listId = `${id}-list`;
  const errorId = `${id}-error`;
  const listRef = useRef<HTMLDivElement>(null);

  const [open, setOpen] = useState(false);
  const [touched, setTouched] = useState(false);
  const [active, setActive] = useState(-1);
  const [scrollTop, setScrollTop] = useState(0);

  // Normalize once per options update, not once per keystroke.
  // This string-based API treats normalized duplicates as one option.
  const index = useMemo(() => {
    const entries = new Map<string, string>();
    for (const option of options) {
      const key = normalize(option);
      if (key && !entries.has(key)) entries.set(key, option);
    }
    return {
      rows: [...entries].map(([key, label]) => ({ key, label })),
      keys: new Set(entries.keys()),
    };
  }, [options]);

  const query = normalize(value);
  const matches = useMemo(
    () =>
      query
        ? index.rows.filter((option) => option.key.includes(query))
        : [],
    [index, query],
  );

  const error =
    !query
      ? required
        ? "This field is required."
        : validate?.(value)
      : !index.keys.has(query)
        ? "Choose a listed option."
        : validate?.(value);

  const invalid = Boolean(error && (touched || value.length > 0));
  const visible = open && !disabled && matches.length > 0;
  const height = Math.min(MAX_HEIGHT, matches.length * ROW_HEIGHT);

  // Reset navigation and scrolling when the query/options change.
  useLayoutEffect(() => {
    setActive(-1);
    setScrollTop(0);
    if (listRef.current) listRef.current.scrollTop = 0;
  }, [matches]);

  const maxStart = Math.max(
    0,
    matches.length - Math.ceil(height / ROW_HEIGHT),
  );
  const start = Math.min(
    maxStart,
    Math.max(0, Math.floor(scrollTop / ROW_HEIGHT) - OVERSCAN),
  );
  const end = Math.min(
    matches.length,
    start + Math.ceil(height / ROW_HEIGHT) + OVERSCAN * 2,
  );

  // Keep the active option mounted for aria-activedescendant.
  const renderedIndexes = Array.from(
    { length: end - start },
    (_, offset) => start + offset,
  );
  if (
    active >= 0 &&
    active < matches.length &&
    !renderedIndexes.includes(active)
  ) {
    renderedIndexes.push(active);
    renderedIndexes.sort((a, b) => a - b);
  }

  useLayoutEffect(() => {
    const list = listRef.current;
    if (!visible || !list || active < 0) return;

    const top = active * ROW_HEIGHT;
    if (top < list.scrollTop) list.scrollTop = top;
    else if (top + ROW_HEIGHT > list.scrollTop + height) {
      list.scrollTop = top + ROW_HEIGHT - height;
    }
    setScrollTop(list.scrollTop);
  }, [active, visible, height]);

  function select(index: number) {
    const option = matches[index];
    if (!option) return;
    onChange(option.label);
    setTouched(true);
    setOpen(false);
    setActive(-1);
  }

  return (
    <div style={{ position: "relative", width: 320 }}>
      <label htmlFor={id}>{label}</label>

      <input
        id={id}
        role="combobox"
        aria-autocomplete="list"
        aria-expanded={visible}
        aria-controls={visible ? listId : undefined}
        aria-activedescendant={
          visible && active >= 0 && active < matches.length
            ? `${listId}-${active}`
            : undefined
        }
        aria-invalid={invalid}
        aria-describedby={invalid ? errorId : undefined}
        autoComplete="off"
        required={required}
        disabled={disabled}
        value={value}
        onFocus={() => setOpen(true)}
        onBlur={() => {
          setTouched(true);
          setOpen(false);
          setActive(-1);
        }}
        onChange={(event) => {
          onChange(event.target.value);
          setActive(-1);
          setOpen(true);
        }}
        onKeyDown={(event) => {
          // Do not intercept IME composition confirmation.
          if (event.nativeEvent.isComposing) return;

          if (event.key === "Escape") {
            if (visible) event.preventDefault();
            setOpen(false);
            setActive(-1);
          } else if (
            matches.length &&
            (event.key === "ArrowDown" || event.key === "ArrowUp")
          ) {
            event.preventDefault();
            setOpen(true);
            const current = visible ? active : -1;
            setActive(
              event.key === "ArrowDown"
                ? (current + 1) % matches.length
                : current <= 0
                  ? matches.length - 1
                  : current - 1,
            );
          } else if (
            event.key === "Enter" &&
            visible &&
            active >= 0
          ) {
            event.preventDefault();
            select(active);
          }
          // Tab retains its normal browser behavior.
        }}
        style={{
          display: "block",
          width: "100%",
          boxSizing: "border-box",
          padding: 8,
          border: `1px solid ${invalid ? "red" : "#888"}`,
          borderRadius: 4,
        }}
      />

      {visible && (
        <div
          ref={listRef}
          id={listId}
          role="listbox"
          aria-label={`${label} suggestions`}
          onScroll={(event) => setScrollTop(event.currentTarget.scrollTop)}
          style={{
            position: "absolute",
            top: "100%",
            left: 0,
            right: 0,
            height,
            overflowY: "auto",
            background: "white",
            border: "1px solid #888",
            zIndex: 10,
          }}
        >
          <div
            role="presentation"
            style={{
              position: "relative",
              height: matches.length * ROW_HEIGHT,
            }}
          >
            {renderedIndexes.map((index) => (
              <div
                key={matches[index].key}
                id={`${listId}-${index}`}
                role="option"
                aria-selected={active === index}
                aria-posinset={index + 1}
                aria-setsize={matches.length}
                // Keep focus on the input until selection completes.
                onMouseDown={(event) => event.preventDefault()}
                onClick={() => select(index)}
                style={{
                  position: "absolute",
                  top: index * ROW_HEIGHT,
                  height: ROW_HEIGHT,
                  width: "100%",
                  boxSizing: "border-box",
                  padding: "8px",
                  overflow: "hidden",
                  whiteSpace: "nowrap",
                  textOverflow: "ellipsis",
                  background: active === index ? "#def" : "white",
                  cursor: "pointer",
                }}
              >
                {matches[index].label}
              </div>
            ))}
          </div>
        </div>
      )}

      {invalid && (
        <div id={errorId} style={{ color: "red" }}>
          {error}
        </div>
      )}
    </div>
  );
}
```

