The main memory cost is storing **26 pointers plus a separate vector allocation in every node**, even when most child links are unused.

A practical improvement is to use:
- A flat `vector<Node>` as a node pool.
- Integer indices instead of pointers.
- Iterative traversal instead of recursion.
- References when iterating over `words`, avoiding string copies.

```cpp
#include <string>
#include <vector>
using namespace std;

class Solution {
    struct Node {
        int child[26]{}; // 0 means missing; root is never a child
        int cnt = 0;
    };

public:
    vector<int> sumPrefixScores(vector<string>& words) {
        vector<Node> nodes(1); // Root at index 0

        for (const string& word : words) {
            int cur = 0;

            for (char ch : word) {
                int c = ch - 'a';
                int next = nodes[cur].child[c];

                if (next == 0) {
                    next = static_cast<int>(nodes.size());
                    nodes.emplace_back();

                    // Access by index after emplace_back:
                    // vector reallocation can invalidate references.
                    nodes[cur].child[c] = next;
                }

                cur = next;
                ++nodes[cur].cnt;
            }
        }

        vector<int> answer;
        answer.reserve(words.size());

        for (const string& word : words) {
            int cur = 0;
            int score = 0;

            // Every queried word was inserted, so its path exists.
            for (char ch : word) {
                cur = nodes[cur].child[ch - 'a'];
                score += nodes[cur].cnt;
            }

            answer.push_back(score);
        }

        return answer;
    }
};
```

On a typical 64-bit system with 4-byte `int`:
- Your original representation takes approximately **240 bytes per node**, before allocator overhead.
- This representation takes approximately **108 bytes per node**, before unused vector capacity.

Additional considerations:
- The pool automatically releases its memory. Your original code never deletes its allocated nodes.
- Vector growth can temporarily hold both old and new buffers. Reserving `1 + sum(word lengths)` avoids growth, but can waste memory when many prefixes are shared.
- If memory is still tight, sparse child storage or a compressed trie can save more, at the cost of additional implementation complexity.
- This assumes lowercase English letters and node counts/scores that fit in `int`, as the original code does.

