### My answer to the prompt

Should the sum **stay within the budget**, or can it exceed the budget if that makes it closer?

If “budget” means **do not exceed `b`**, use this function. It selects one integer from each array and returns the largest sum at most `b`, or `null` if no valid pair exists:

```javascript
function getExpense(arr1, arr2, b) {
  let best = null;

  for (const x of arr1) {
    for (const y of arr2) {
      const sum = x + y;

      if (sum === b) return sum;

      if (sum <= b && (best === null || sum > best)) {
        best = sum;
      }
    }
  }

  return best;
}

console.log(getExpense([40, 50, 60], [5, 8, 12], 58)); // 58
```

This handles negative integers too. Time complexity is **O(n × m)**, with **O(1)** extra space.

If exceeding the budget is allowed, replace the update condition with:

```javascript
if (best === null || Math.abs(b - sum) < Math.abs(b - best)) {
  best = sum;
}
```

This version keeps the first encountered sum when two sums are equally close.

