## My answer to the user prompt

Do you mean **copy the object’s actual memory**, or **encode its contents in a compact binary format**? Also, is this for native interop, storage, or transfer to another process?

A `HashSet<string>` cannot be meaningfully converted to self-contained bytes with a raw memory copy. It contains references to internal arrays and strings; copying the object’s memory would not copy those referenced objects, and its layout is runtime-dependent.

If you want low-level control over the **byte format**, use explicit binary serialization:

```csharp
using System;
using System.Collections.Generic;
using System.IO;
using System.Text;

public static class StringSetEncoding
{
    public static byte[] ToBytes(HashSet<string> set)
    {
        if (set == null)
            throw new ArgumentNullException(nameof(set));

        using var stream = new MemoryStream();
        using (var writer = new BinaryWriter(
            stream,
            new UTF8Encoding(false, true),
            leaveOpen: true))
        {
            writer.Write((byte)1); // Format version
            writer.Write(set.Count);

            foreach (var value in set)
            {
                writer.Write(value != null);
                if (value != null)
                    writer.Write(value);
            }
        }

        return stream.ToArray();
    }
}

// Usage:
var set = new HashSet<string> { "apple", "banana" };
byte[] bytes = StringSetEncoding.ToBytes(set);
```

**Format:** one version byte, a little-endian 32-bit element count, then each element represented by a one-byte presence flag and, if present, a UTF-8 string with `BinaryWriter`’s 7-bit-encoded byte-length prefix.

This encodes the contents, **not the internal hash table or equality comparer**. A reader must use the same format and an agreed comparer. Enumeration order is not guaranteed; sort with an explicit ordinal comparer first if deterministic bytes matter. The strict UTF-8 encoder rejects malformed surrogate sequences; use an explicit UTF-16-code-unit format if those must round-trip.

Avoid `BinaryFormatter`: it is insecure and unsupported in modern .NET.

