A `HashSet<string>` cannot be copied as a self-contained memory block: it contains references to internal arrays and strings, and its layout is runtime-dependent. Pinning it or copying its reference does not solve that.

If you want explicit control over the bytes, use a small custom binary format:

```csharp
using System;
using System.Collections.Generic;
using System.IO;
using System.Text;

public static class HashSetEncoding
{
    // Format:
    //   byte  version = 1
    //   int32 element count
    //   For each element:
    //     int32 UTF-8 byte length (-1 means null)
    //     byte[length] UTF-8 data
    //
    // Integers are little-endian.
    public static byte[] ToBytes(HashSet<string> set)
    {
        if (set == null)
            throw new ArgumentNullException(nameof(set));

        // Reject malformed UTF-16 instead of silently replacing it.
        var utf8 = new UTF8Encoding(false, true);

        using var stream = new MemoryStream();
        using var writer = new BinaryWriter(stream, utf8, leaveOpen: true);

        writer.Write((byte)1);
        writer.Write(set.Count);

        foreach (string value in set)
        {
            if (value == null)
            {
                writer.Write(-1);
                continue;
            }

            byte[] data = utf8.GetBytes(value);
            writer.Write(data.Length);
            writer.Write(data);
        }

        writer.Flush();
        return stream.ToArray();
    }
}
```

```csharp
byte[] bytes = HashSetEncoding.ToBytes(
    new HashSet<string> { "apple", "banana", "cherry" });
```

This encodes the **elements**, not the set’s internal buckets or equality comparer. To reconstruct equivalent membership behavior, use the same comparer when decoding. Enumeration order is unspecified; sort a snapshot with a specified comparer only if you need deterministic bytes. Do not modify the set during serialization.

For untrusted input, a decoder should validate counts and lengths, impose allocation limits, and reject truncated data. Avoid `BinaryFormatter`.

