## My answer to the prompt

Use a discriminated union so TypeScript knows whether the result contains a value or an error:

```typescript
export type Result<T, E = unknown> =
  | { readonly ok: true; readonly value: T }
  | { readonly ok: false; readonly error: E };

/** Execute a synchronous callback and capture anything it throws. */
export function tryCatch<T>(fn: () => T): Result<T> {
  try {
    return { ok: true, value: fn() };
  } catch (error: unknown) {
    return { ok: false, error };
  }
}

/** Execute a callback and capture synchronous throws or promise rejections. */
export async function tryCatchAsync<T>(
  fn: () => T | PromiseLike<T>,
): Promise<Result<Awaited<T>>> {
  try {
    return { ok: true, value: await fn() };
  } catch (error: unknown) {
    return { ok: false, error };
  }
}
```

Example:

```typescript
const result = tryCatch(() => {
  if (Math.random() < 0.5) throw new Error("Operation failed");
  return 42;
});

if (result.ok) {
  console.log(result.value); // number
} else {
  console.error(
    result.error instanceof Error
      ? result.error.message
      : String(result.error),
  );
}
```

Errors are `unknown` because JavaScript can throw any value. Use `tryCatchAsync` for promise-returning callbacks; the synchronous helper does not catch later promise rejections.

This interprets “wrap” as executing a supplied callback immediately. If you mean returning a reusable decorated function that preserves its parameters and `this`, that requires a different signature.

