## My answer to the user prompt

Two details need clarification before finalizing the project:

1. Are you using a **classic Arduino Nano (ATmega328P)** or a **Raspberry Pi Pico**? You mention both; their wiring and software differ.
2. Does “reduce nighttime to 1 hour” mean **shorten the overnight sleep period**, or **shorten the bedroom TV session from two simulated hours to one**?

Below is a working starting point assuming a **classic Nano**, a **DS3231 RTC**, and a **128×64 SSD1306 I²C OLED**. I interpret shortened nighttime as reducing the *sleep-only period* to one simulated hour, retaining your requested two hours of bedroom TV. Confirm your RTC and OLED models before wiring.

### 1. How it works

- The RTC continues keeping normal time.
- Software multiplies elapsed RTC seconds by an adjustable ratio.
- At **6:1, one real second equals six simulated seconds**. A complete simulated day takes four real hours.
- House lights follow a daily schedule.
- Separate PWM outputs produce irregular brightness changes for both TVs.
- The OLED shows simulated time, event, and speed.
- A switch optionally skips most of the sleep-only period.
- The schedule repeats until power is removed.

### 2. Parts and wiring

Use five ordinary LEDs initially: three room lights and two blue or cool-white TV LEDs.

| Nano pin | Connection |
|---|---|
| A4 | RTC SDA and OLED SDA |
| A5 | RTC SCL and OLED SCL |
| D2 | Kitchen LED |
| D4 | Living-room LED |
| D7 | Bedroom LED |
| D5 | Living-room TV LED—PWM |
| D6 | Bedroom TV LED—PWM |
| D8 | Short-night switch; other terminal to GND |
| GND | All circuit grounds |

For each test LED, wire:

**Output pin → 470 Ω resistor → LED anode; LED cathode → GND.**

This keeps individual LED currents modest. For multiple LEDs or higher-current lighting, use appropriately rated transistor/MOSFET drivers, current limiting, and a suitable external low-voltage supply with common ground. Do not drive lighting loads or relay coils directly from Nano pins.

Power RTC/OLED modules only at voltages their specific boards support. A bare 3.3 V-only module must not be exposed to 5 V I²C pull-ups. Also check whether your DS3231 board has a coin-cell charging circuit; do not charge a non-rechargeable CR2032.

### 3. Example schedule

All times are simulated.

| Time | Activity |
|---|---|
| 06:00–07:00 | Kitchen/bedroom lights; bedroom morning-news TV |
| 07:00–08:00 | Breakfast; kitchen light |
| 08:00–17:00 | Away; everything off |
| 17:00–19:00 | Dinner; kitchen and living-room lights |
| 19:00–21:30 | Living-room lights and TV |
| 21:30–22:00 | Bedroom light |
| 22:00–00:00 | Bedroom TV only; all room lights off |
| 00:00–06:00 | Sleep; everything off |

With short-night selected, the clock jumps from **01:00 to 06:00**, making the sleep-only period one simulated hour. That compressed day takes **3 hours 10 minutes of real time** at 6:1.

### 4. Install libraries and upload

In Arduino IDE Library Manager, install:

- **RTClib** by Adafruit
- **U8g2** by olikraus

The sketch uses U8g2’s **U8x8 text-only mode**, which avoids a full-screen framebuffer and saves the Nano’s limited RAM.

```cpp
#include <Wire.h>
#include <RTClib.h>
#include <U8x8lib.h>

RTC_DS3231 rtc;
U8X8_SSD1306_128X64_NONAME_HW_I2C oled(U8X8_PIN_NONE);

const byte KITCHEN = 2;
const byte LIVING = 4;
const byte BEDROOM = 7;
const byte LIVING_TV = 5;
const byte BEDROOM_TV = 6;
const byte SHORT_NIGHT = 8;

// Change this and upload again to change the ratio.
const uint16_t SPEED = 6;

uint32_t simSeconds = 6UL * 3600UL;
uint32_t lastRtcSeconds;
uint32_t lastScreen = 0;

const char* eventName = "Starting";

struct TV {
  byte pin;
  uint32_t lastChange;
  uint16_t interval;
  bool running;
};

TV livingTV = {LIVING_TV, 0, 0, false};
TV bedroomTV = {BEDROOM_TV, 0, 0, false};

void updateTV(TV &tv, bool enabled, uint32_t nowMs) {
  if (!enabled) {
    analogWrite(tv.pin, 0);
    tv.running = false;
    return;
  }

  if (!tv.running ||
      (uint32_t)(nowMs - tv.lastChange) >= tv.interval) {
    tv.running = true;
    tv.lastChange = nowMs;
    tv.interval = (uint16_t)random(70, 351);

    // Mostly changing brightness, with occasional dark cuts.
    byte brightness = random(12) == 0
                      ? 0 : (byte)random(35, 256);
    analogWrite(tv.pin, brightness);
  }
}

void advanceSimulation(uint32_t seconds, bool shortNight) {
  while (seconds--) {
    simSeconds++;
    if (simSeconds >= 86400UL) simSeconds = 0;

    // Keep the two-hour bedtime TV session.
    // Compress only the subsequent sleep-only period.
    if (shortNight &&
        simSeconds >= 3600UL &&
        simSeconds < 6UL * 3600UL) {
      simSeconds = 6UL * 3600UL;
    }
  }
}

void applySchedule(uint32_t nowMs) {
  uint16_t minuteOfDay = simSeconds / 60UL;
  bool kitchen = false, living = false, bedroom = false;
  bool livingTvOn = false, bedroomTvOn = false;

  if (minuteOfDay < 360) {
    eventName = "Sleep";
  } else if (minuteOfDay < 420) {
    eventName = "Morning news";
    kitchen = bedroom = bedroomTvOn = true;
  } else if (minuteOfDay < 480) {
    eventName = "Breakfast";
    kitchen = true;
  } else if (minuteOfDay < 1020) {
    eventName = "Away";
  } else if (minuteOfDay < 1140) {
    eventName = "Dinner";
    kitchen = living = true;
  } else if (minuteOfDay < 1290) {
    eventName = "Living room TV";
    living = livingTvOn = true;
  } else if (minuteOfDay < 1320) {
    eventName = "Getting ready";
    bedroom = true;
  } else {
    eventName = "Bedtime TV";
    bedroomTvOn = true;
  }

  digitalWrite(KITCHEN, kitchen);
  digitalWrite(LIVING, living);
  digitalWrite(BEDROOM, bedroom);
  updateTV(livingTV, livingTvOn, nowMs);
  updateTV(bedroomTV, bedroomTvOn, nowMs);
}

void setup() {
  const byte outputs[] = {
    KITCHEN, LIVING, BEDROOM, LIVING_TV, BEDROOM_TV
  };

  for (byte i = 0; i < sizeof(outputs); i++) {
    pinMode(outputs[i], OUTPUT);
    digitalWrite(outputs[i], LOW);
  }

  pinMode(SHORT_NIGHT, INPUT_PULLUP);
  Wire.begin();

  oled.begin();
  oled.setFont(u8x8_font_chroma48medium8_r);
  oled.clearDisplay();

  if (!rtc.begin()) {
    oled.drawString(0, 0, "RTC missing");
    while (true) {}
  }

  if (rtc.lostPower()) {
    rtc.adjust(DateTime(F(__DATE__), F(__TIME__)));
  }

  lastRtcSeconds = rtc.now().unixtime();
  randomSeed(analogRead(A0));  // Leave A0 unconnected.
}

void loop() {
  uint32_t realSeconds = rtc.now().unixtime();
  bool shortNight = digitalRead(SHORT_NIGHT) == LOW;

  if (realSeconds != lastRtcSeconds) {
    uint32_t elapsed = realSeconds - lastRtcSeconds;
    lastRtcSeconds = realSeconds;
    advanceSimulation(elapsed * (uint32_t)SPEED, shortNight);
  }

  uint32_t nowMs = millis();
  applySchedule(nowMs);

  if ((uint32_t)(nowMs - lastScreen) >= 250UL) {
    lastScreen = nowMs;
    char line[17];

    snprintf(line, sizeof(line), "%02u:%02u:%02u",
             (unsigned)(simSeconds / 3600UL),
             (unsigned)((simSeconds / 60UL) % 60UL),
             (unsigned)(simSeconds % 60UL));
    oled.drawString(0, 0, line);

    snprintf(line, sizeof(line), "%-16s", eventName);
    oled.drawString(0, 2, line);

    snprintf(line, sizeof(line), "Speed %u:1      ",
             (unsigned)SPEED);
    oled.drawString(0, 4, line);

    oled.drawString(0, 6, shortNight
      ? "Short sleep: ON " : "Short sleep: OFF");
  }
}
```

This version starts at 06:00 whenever powered on; it does not resume the previous simulated time. The RTC supplies elapsed time, not the displayed time of day. Do not adjust the RTC while the simulation is running.

The clock advances in six-second steps at 6:1 because the RTC is sampled at one-second resolution. TV flickering runs independently in real time.

### 5. Test it

1. Test each LED individually before connecting everything.
2. Upload with `SPEED = 60` temporarily: a normal day then lasts 24 real minutes.
3. Verify both TVs operate in their correct rooms.
4. Confirm every room light is off during bedtime TV.
5. Close the D8 switch and verify the 01:00 → 06:00 jump.
6. Restore `SPEED = 6`.

If you instead mean **one hour of bedtime TV**, retain the normal overnight clock progression and end bedtime TV at 23:00 when the switch is closed.

