## My answer to the prompt

The order of the elements matters: we cannot necessarily keep the largest elements.

Let
\[
m=\left\lfloor\frac{n-1}{k}\right\rfloor,\qquad r=n-mk.
\]
Exactly \(m\) operations are performed, leaving \(r\) elements.

### Which elements can remain?

Suppose the remaining elements have original indices
\[
i_1<i_2<\cdots<i_r.
\]

Every gap before, between, or after these indices must contain a multiple of \(k\) deleted elements. Indeed, an operation cannot cross an element that survives. Conversely, if every gap has length divisible by \(k\), we can delete each gap in blocks of size \(k\).

Therefore, the remaining indices have exactly the form
\[
i_j=j+kt_j,\qquad 0\le t_1\le t_2\le\cdots\le t_r\le m.
\]

### Check a candidate median

For a candidate value \(x\), mark each element as good if it is at least \(x\).

The lower median of \(r\) elements is at least \(x\) precisely when at least
\[
\left\lfloor\frac r2\right\rfloor+1
\]
remaining elements are good.

We use dynamic programming to maximize that count.

For each survivor number \(j\), let `dp[t]` represent the maximum number of good elements when its index is \(j+kt\). The transition is
\[
dp_{\text{new}}[t]
=
\max_{0\le u\le t}dp_{\text{old}}[u]
+
[a_{j+kt}\ge x].
\]

A running prefix maximum evaluates one layer in \(O(m+1)\). There are \(r\) layers, and
\[
r(m+1)\le mk+r=n,
\]
so each check takes \(O(n)\).

Binary-search the largest feasible value.

### C++17 solution

```cpp
#include <bits/stdc++.h>
using namespace std;

int main() {
    ios::sync_with_stdio(false);
    cin.tie(nullptr);

    int T;
    cin >> T;

    while (T--) {
        int n, k;
        cin >> n >> k;

        vector<int> a(n);
        for (int &v : a) cin >> v;

        int m = (n - 1) / k;
        int r = n - m * k;
        int need = r / 2 + 1;

        vector<int> dp(m + 1);

        auto feasible = [&](int x) -> bool {
            fill(dp.begin(), dp.end(), 0);

            for (int j = 1; j <= r; ++j) {
                int prefixBest = 0;

                for (int t = 0; t <= m; ++t) {
                    // dp[t] still belongs to the previous layer here.
                    prefixBest = max(prefixBest, dp[t]);
                    int index = (j - 1) + k * t;
                    dp[t] = prefixBest + (a[index] >= x);
                }
            }

            return *max_element(dp.begin(), dp.end()) >= need;
        };

        int low = *min_element(a.begin(), a.end());
        int high = *max_element(a.begin(), a.end());

        while (low < high) {
            int mid = low + (high - low + 1) / 2;

            if (feasible(mid))
                low = mid;
            else
                high = mid - 1;
        }

        cout << low << '\n';
    }
}
```

**Complexity:** \(O(n\log V)\) time per test case, where \(V\le10^9\), and \(O(n)\) space.

