## Correct solution

The original order matters: we cannot necessarily keep the largest elements.

For example, with `a = [1, 100, 2]` and `k = 2`, the only possible final arrays are `[1]` and `[2]`. Thus the answer is `2`, not `100`.

### 1. Characterize the possible remaining arrays

The number of operations and final length are fixed:
\[
m=\left\lfloor\frac{n-1}{k}\right\rfloor,\qquad r=n-mk.
\]

Let the surviving elements have original indices \(i_1<i_2<\cdots<i_r\), using 1-based indexing.

Every gap before, between, or after surviving elements must contain a multiple of \(k\) deleted elements. This is necessary because no deletion can cross an element that survives. It is also sufficient: delete each gap in blocks of size \(k\).

Consequently, precisely the valid survivor sequences have
\[
i_j=j+k t_j,\qquad 0\le t_1\le t_2\le\cdots\le t_r\le m.
\]

### 2. Check a candidate median

For a candidate value \(x\), give each element score `1` if it is at least \(x\), otherwise `0`.

The lower median of \(r\) elements is at least \(x\) exactly when at least
\[
\left\lfloor\frac r2\right\rfloor+1
\]
of them have score `1`.

We maximize this count with dynamic programming.

Process \(t=0,1,\ldots,m\). Let `dp[j]` represent the largest score for choosing the first \(j\) survivors using the processed values of \(t\).

For each \(t\), update \(j=1,\ldots,r\) in increasing order:
```cpp
dp[j] = max(dp[j], dp[j - 1] + (a[t * k + j - 1] >= x));
```

Keeping the old value skips this candidate; taking the transition selects it. Increasing `j` allows consecutive survivors to use the same \(t\).

Each check examines \((m+1)r\le n\) candidates, so it takes \(O(n)\) time. Binary-search the distinct input values.

### C++17 code

```cpp
#include <bits/stdc++.h>
using namespace std;

int main() {
    ios::sync_with_stdio(false);
    cin.tie(nullptr);

    int T;
    cin >> T;

    while (T--) {
        int n, k;
        cin >> n >> k;

        vector<int> a(n);
        for (int &v : a) cin >> v;

        int m = (n - 1) / k;
        int r = n - m * k;

        vector<int> values = a;
        sort(values.begin(), values.end());
        values.erase(unique(values.begin(), values.end()), values.end());

        vector<int> dp(r + 1);
        const int NEG = -1000000000;

        auto possible = [&](int x) {
            fill(dp.begin(), dp.end(), NEG);
            dp[0] = 0;

            for (int t = 0; t <= m; ++t) {
                int base = t * k;
                for (int j = 1; j <= r; ++j) {
                    if (dp[j - 1] != NEG) {
                        int score = (a[base + j - 1] >= x);
                        dp[j] = max(dp[j], dp[j - 1] + score);
                    }
                }
            }

            return dp[r] >= r / 2 + 1;
        };

        int lo = 0;
        int hi = static_cast<int>(values.size()) - 1;

        while (lo < hi) {
            int mid = lo + (hi - lo + 1) / 2;
            if (possible(values[mid])) {
                lo = mid;
            } else {
                hi = mid - 1;
            }
        }

        cout << values[lo] << '\n';
    }
}
```

**Complexity:** \(O(n\log n)\) time and \(O(n)\) extra space per test case.

